# Everything is excluded, then the things the images actually need are allowed
# back. A deny-list here would silently start shipping whatever gets added to
# the repository next; this cannot.
#
# ⚠ **One context, two images.** Both Dockerfiles build from the repository
# root, so this file governs the router as well as the API. It was written as
# though only the API existed, and the router build failed on its first ever
# run with `COPY failed: graphhopper/config.yml: file does not exist` — the
# blanket `graphhopper/` exclusion below had taken the router's own config with
# it. Nothing caught it, because Phase 3's images were written and never built.
*

!backend/
!data/cache.db

# ...and then the parts of backend/ that must not ship.
backend/tests/
backend/**/__pycache__/
backend/**/*.py[cod]
backend/requirements.txt

# Named explicitly as well as excluded by the * above, because each one is a
# specific hazard rather than mere weight:
#
#   fixtures/     ~150 recorded upstream responses, none of them needed at
#                 runtime — live mode never reads them, and Phase 1.3 stopped
#                 production writing them.
#   graphhopper/  a 45 MB JAR and a multi-GB graph. The router has its own
#                 image; putting the graph in the API image would be a
#                 gigabyte of dead weight per deploy. Three small files are
#                 allowed back below — they are the router image's inputs.
#   frontend/     served from S3, not from here.
#   .env          secrets. config.py loads it if present, so an .env that
#                 reached the image would silently override the task
#                 definition's environment.
fixtures/
graphhopper/
frontend/
scripts/
infra/
docs/
.env
.env.*
.venv*/
.git/
.github/
.claude/
*.md
data/*.db-wal
data/*.db-shm

# The router image's own inputs, allowed back after the blanket `graphhopper/`
# above. Order matters here: a later rule wins, so these must follow it.
#
# The JAR and graphhopper/data/ stay excluded. The JAR is downloaded and
# checksummed inside the image, and the graph is a build artifact staged into
# graph-dist/ by scripts/publish_graph.sh — which is empty except when someone
# is deliberately baking a graph in. The API image never COPYs any of these;
# they cost it a little context-transfer time and nothing in the image.
!graphhopper/config.yml
!graphhopper/docker-entrypoint.sh
!graphhopper/graph-dist/
graphhopper/data/
graphhopper/*.jar
